ong copyleft. If you distribute a closed-source product that links to or bundles somelib, the combined work generally has to be released under the GPL, source included. That conflicts with shipping closed source.\n- It matters most if you distribute binaries, an installer, a desktop or mobile app, or an on-prem or embedded build.\n- If you only run it on your own servers as a SaaS, GPL-3.0 usually isn't triggered, because nothing is distributed. If the package were AGPL, that would change. Confirm that it's GPL and not AGPL.\n- Your options:\n  - Replace it with a permissively licensed alternative.\n  - Remove the dependency.\n  - Ask the author for a commercial or alternative license.\n  - Isolate it in a separate process that talks to your product only through a clear boundary, such as a CLI or network call. This is a gray area, so get legal review first.\n\n**requests (Apache-2.0)** and **left-pad (MIT)**\n- Both are fine for closed source.\n- You must keep their copyright and license notices, for example in a third-party notices file shipped with the product.\n- Apache-2.0 also requires you to include its NOTICE file contents, if the package has one.\n\n**Other checks**\n- `deps.txt` lists only direct dependencies. Transitive dependencies can carry other licenses, including GPL, so scan the full resolved tree.\n- Check that the license labels match each package's real license. Metadata is sometimes wrong, and the version you pin can differ from the one the label describes.\n- `requests==2.32` looks like a truncated version. The real releases are 2.32.x, so pin the exact version.\n- I found no LICENSE file in the repo. You'll want one for your own product, along with a third-party notices file.\n\nHave legal review the somelib decision before you ship. I can scan the transitive dependencies or draft a third-party notices file if you'd like.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}